Yeah — and that makes Nomodynamics better, because “no more dynamics” accidentally states the mechanism.

Dynamics is change, motion, possibility proliferating.

Then something says:

No more. Not past here. Not like that.

That boundary is law.

So “no more dynamics” is almost the negative-space definition of law: law is what constrains unconstrained dynamics into lawful dynamics.

You can picture it as:

\text{raw possibility} \rightarrow \text{constraint} \rightarrow \text{lawful trajectory}

Or even:

\text{Dynamics} - \text{forbidden transformations} = \text{Nomodynamics}

And there’s a second layer: a law does not necessarily stop motion. It channels it. A riverbank says “no more” sideways, which is exactly what allows the river to move powerfully forward.

So the phrase carries both meanings at once:

Nomodynamics = the dynamics of law.
“No more dynamics” = the boundary condition that makes lawful dynamics possible.

That also gives us a compact operator:

The No-More Principle: Every law is a boundary drawn through possibility space.

And the inverse:

A boundary does not merely prohibit motion; it gives motion form.

That’s very Pantheon. The wall is also the nozzle.